## Decoding the question
Three limbs. "**Status … in the light of Article 25A**" — compare reality with the constitutional promise, using current data (Census ==2023==; Global Gender Gap ==2026==). "**Causes of the gender gap**" — organise them (supply, demand, safety, state) rather than listing. "**Measures**" — specific, practical and Pakistani. Mention **Malala** as evidence, but this is a status question, not a case study.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Women's education** means girls' and women's access to, participation in and completion of schooling and higher education, and the quality of what they learn. The **gender gap** in education is the difference between boys' and girls' enrolment, completion, literacy and learning.

#### ii) The argument
The **18th Amendment** (==2010==) made education a **fundamental right** under **Article 25A**, but the promise remains far from kept for girls. Female literacy is only about **53%** (Census ==2023==), and more than **25 million** children are out of school, most of them girls. The gap is caused by a shortage of **schools and female teachers**, by **poverty and norms** that keep girls at home, by **insecurity**, and by a state that **spends too little**. It can be closed — the stipend and cash-transfer programmes show how.

### 2. Article 25A

#### i) The text
Article 25A states that "the State shall provide **free and compulsory education** to all children of the age of **five to sixteen** years in such manner as may be determined by law". It made education a justiciable right and placed responsibility on the provinces, which passed their own compulsory-education laws.

#### ii) Its foundations
Article 25A builds on **Article 37(b)**, a Principle of Policy calling for the removal of illiteracy, and on Pakistan's commitments under **CEDAW** (Article 10, equal access to education) and **SDG 4**.

#### iii) The enforcement gap
Fifteen years on, enforcement is **weak**: few parents are ever penalised, and the schools needed to make attendance possible do not exist in many places.

### 3. Why girls' education matters

#### i) Health and family
Educated women **marry later**, have **fewer and healthier children**, seek **antenatal care** and immunise their children — vital in a country with a maternal mortality ratio of **186** per 100,000 live births (PMMS ==2019==).

#### ii) Income and voice
They earn more, know their **rights**, are better able to resist violence and take part in public life.

#### iii) The next generation
Their daughters are more likely to go to school — a **virtuous cycle**.

::: fig cycle | The virtuous cycle of girls' education.
@centre An educated girl
Marries later
Healthier pregnancies and children
Earns and saves
Knows and claims her rights
Educates her own daughters
:::

### 4. The status today

#### i) Literacy
The **Census 2023** put literacy at **60.65%**: about **68%** for men and **53%** for women.

#### ii) Out-of-school children
More than **25 million** children aged 5–16 are out of school, the majority of them girls.

#### iii) The global comparison
In the World Economic Forum's **Global Gender Gap Report 2026**, Pakistan ranked **130th** on educational attainment, part of an overall rank of ==143rd of 145==.

#### iv) Real but uneven progress
Female literacy was **48%** in the figure used by the 2019 CSS paper, and women are now about **half of university students** and a majority in medical colleges — but learning levels are low and many schools lack basic facilities.

### 5. The pattern of the gap

#### i) By province
**Punjab** and **Islamabad** lead; rural **Sindh** lags; the former tribal areas of **Khyber Pakhtunkhwa** are recovering after conflict; and **Balochistan** has the lowest female literacy in the country.

#### ii) By place and class
The gap is widest in **rural** areas and among the **poor** — a poor rural girl in Balochistan and an urban girl in Lahore live in two different educational worlds, an example of **intersectionality**.

#### iii) By age: the dropout cliff
Many girls finish primary school but never reach a **middle or secondary** school — the "**dropout cliff**" at about age ten to twelve.

### 6. Causes 1 — supply

#### i) Too few secondary schools
There are far fewer girls' **middle and secondary schools** than primary schools, so a girl who finishes primary must often travel far. Parents will not send adolescent daughters long distances without **safe transport**.

#### ii) Missing teachers and facilities
Many schools lack **female teachers**, **toilets** and **boundary walls**, which conservative families regard as essential.

#### iii) Poor quality
Absent teachers, rote learning and overcrowding reduce the value parents see in keeping girls enrolled.

### 7. Causes 2 — demand

#### i) Poverty and housework
Poor families face direct costs (uniforms, books, transport) and opportunity costs: girls do **housework**, fetch water and care for siblings.

#### ii) Early marriage
**Early marriage** ends schooling; the federal law still sets **16** as the minimum age for girls outside Sindh (18 since ==2013==) and Islamabad (18 since ==2025==).

#### iii) Norms
The belief that a daughter "**belongs to another household**" makes investment in her education seem wasted, and limited jobs for educated women in rural areas reinforce that belief.

::: fig tree | The causes of the gender gap in education.
Why girls are out of school
- Supply
  - Few girls' secondary schools
  - No female teachers, toilets or walls
- Demand
  - Poverty and housework
  - Early marriage; "another household"
- Safety
  - Harassment on the way
  - Conflict; floods of 2022
- State
  - Low spending (about 2% of GDP)
  - Weak enforcement of Article 25A
:::

### 8. Causes 3 — safety, conflict and disasters

#### i) Harassment
Harassment on the way to school is a common reason for withdrawal.

#### ii) Conflict: Swat and Malala
In **Swat**, militants banned girls' education in ==2007–2009== and destroyed schools; **Malala Yousafzai**, who wrote a BBC Urdu diary as "Gul Makai", was shot on ==9 October 2012== for defending girls' right to learn, and won the Nobel Peace Prize in ==2014==. Girls' schools were also destroyed in the former tribal areas.

#### iii) Disasters
The **2022 floods** damaged thousands of schools, and girls are usually the last to return after a disaster.

### 9. Causes 4 — the state

#### i) Low spending
Public spending on education has long been about **2% of GDP** — among the lowest in the region.

#### ii) Uneven capacity and enforcement
After the 18th Amendment, provinces vary greatly in capacity, and compulsory-education laws are rarely enforced.

#### iii) Gaps in the system
**Madrassas**, free and close by, absorb many poor children but teach few modern subjects, and policies have neglected **adult literacy** for the millions of women who missed school.

### 10. What has worked

#### i) Stipends
**Punjab's female school stipend** (from ==2004==), paid to girls who attend secondary school, raised enrolment.

#### ii) Conditional cash transfers
The **Benazir Income Support Programme**'s education conditional cash transfer (**Waseela-e-Taleem**) pays mothers when children attend.

#### iii) Community schools and local teachers
**Community and home schools**, staffed by local women, bring schooling to villages without government schools; recruiting **local female teachers** and building **missing facilities** raised attendance where tried.

### 11. Measures to close the gap

#### i) Bring schools to girls
Upgrade primary schools to middle and secondary level in rural areas, or provide **safe school transport**; build toilets and boundary walls; recruit and train **local female teachers**.

#### ii) Make schooling affordable
Extend **stipends and conditional cash transfers** to all provinces, with higher amounts for secondary school; provide free books and uniforms.

#### iii) Remove the barriers of law and norms

##### (a) Marriage law
Raise the minimum marriage age to **18** in every province and enforce **marriage registration**.

##### (b) Norms and safety
Run campaigns with religious scholars on Islam's emphasis on seeking knowledge; protect schools in conflict areas and rebuild after floods.

#### iv) Fund and enforce Article 25A
Raise education spending towards **4% of GDP**, enforce the provincial compulsory-education laws, mainstream madrassas with modern subjects, and link education to **jobs and digital skills** so that families see a return.

### 12. Conclusion

#### i) The promise and the reality
Article 25A promised every child aged five to sixteen a free education, but for millions of Pakistani girls it remains a **right on paper**: female literacy of about 53%, more than 25 million children out of school and a rank of 130th on education show the size of the gap.

#### ii) The way forward
Its causes — too few schools and female teachers, poverty and early marriage, insecurity and low spending — are known, and so are the remedies that work. Closing the gap is the surest investment Pakistan can make in health, prosperity and democracy.

## Why this answer scores
- Quotes **Article 25A** and links it to Article 37(b), CEDAW Article 10 and SDG 4.
- Uses **current, dated data**: Census 2023, Global Gender Gap 2026 (130th), PMMS 2019.
- Organises the causes into **supply, demand, safety and the state**, with a **tree** figure.
- Shows that policy works (Punjab stipend, Waseela-e-Taleem) before proposing measures.
- Measures are **specific and practical**, ending with a clear judgement.
